 r"I am writing to apply for the baringa.ai Fellowship at Baringa. I completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London in September 2026, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have built machine learning models, evaluated AI systems against clear standards, and turned the results into decisions that clients and colleagues act on.",

 r"What attracts me most to the Fellowship is that the work has a visible end point: AI applied to live client problems, not research that stays on paper. The posting asks for people who can simplify complex ideas and turn technical depth into something useful quickly, and that is the part of AI work I enjoy most. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I analyse merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python and build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations, product and commercial teams use to track initiatives. When I was asked to configure a merchant campaign by hand, I worked out the logic after a few examples and used Claude Code to build an AI agent with a validation step to check accuracy. It cut the time the task took by around 40 to 50 percent, and the main lesson was that a tool only creates value once the people using it trust it.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern on a medical AI project for a pharmaceutical client. I designed a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension safety rubric with the client's clinical experts, which the client adopted as its acceptance standard, and built a Python pipeline that called six LLM APIs with retrieval and search augmentation and scored more than 2,400 responses using an LLM-as-judge framework. I also helped build a medically reviewed knowledge graph and designed blinded paired A/B tests of its effect. This taught me to define what good looks like before building anything.",

 r"My MSc dissertation used data from 458,840 UK Biobank participants to compare logistic regression with tree-based models for cardiovascular risk prediction, then used doubly robust causal forests to estimate the effect of lifestyle changes, with sensitivity analyses and external validation. I delivered the results as an interactive Streamlit tool, which taught me to explain uncertain results clearly to non-specialists.",

 r"I would bring Baringa strong Python, machine learning and LLM evaluation skills, experience of working directly with clients, and the habit of checking that a model is trustworthy before it is used. I have the right to work in the UK until January 2029 without sponsorship, and I am based in London.",

